{
 "idea": {
  "id": "earn_premium_sharp",
  "family": "earnings",
  "title": "The earnings-report premium in high-attention names, three sharpenings",
  "why": "`earn_premium:over_2d_high_volume_names` earned 24 bp a round trip before costs and gave 9 of them to costs (0.76 discovery, 0.82 validation). Three changes, each with a reason, none a search. (a) Liquidity: the same trade costs a third as much in names trading $50M a day or more; the question is whether the premium is still there, because the story (buyers drawn by attention who cannot easily be met by short sellers) should be weaker where shorting is easy. `mid_only` is the other half, to show where the premium lives. (b) Timing: attention and option buying build over the days before a report (Frazzini and Lamont measure the premium over the whole month), so entering one session earlier should add return for the same cost. (c) Run-up: a stock already up more than 10% in the week before its report has had its attention buying, and Aboody, Lehavy and Trueman (2010) find the strongest pre-report runners give it back over the report.",
  "source": "Frazzini and Lamont 2007; Aboody, Lehavy, Trueman 2010; lab session 2026-11-22",
